00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.310
So with that, let's get started.

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.260
So before we dive
into the amazing work

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.449
that Linda and FarmLogix
has done to date,

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:01.490
I thought it would be
interesting to rewind a bit

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:03.990
and share a brief
history on Earth Day.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.560
So Earth Day began with
an ecological tragedy.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.530
In 1969, over three
million gallons of oil

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.750
spilled off the coast
of Santa Barbara,

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.440
killing over 10,000 animals
in the region, from sea lions

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.990
to dolphins to
seagulls, among others.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.440
Now, by that point, America
had been somewhat primed

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.380
for a new ecological
consciousness with the 1962

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.530
publication of Rachel
Carson's "A Silent Spring."

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:30.230
It was in that work that she
discussed the impact of DDT

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.460
and other pesticides
on the environment

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.510
on all living things, from
crops to the pests that

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:40.790
afflicted those crops to
ecological systems, animals,

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.800
and humans.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.520
Now, at that point, the US
Senator Gaylord Nelson actually

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.940
decided to mobilize on that
new ecological consciousness,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.960
and coming off of the
movements of the 1960s.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.260
And so in 1969, in light
of that ecological tragedy

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.110
in Santa Barbara, he proposed
a televised national teach-in

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.610
on the environment, which
took place on April 22nd.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.260
Millions of Americans
rallied that day

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.380
to support new responsible
environmental practices,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.420
leading to the
creation of the EPA,

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.390
and also clean acts later
enacted by Congress.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:16.600
From there, it grew
into a global movement.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:23.740
So fast forward 40 plus years
to the new and ever-growing

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.230
challenges that we face with
the environment, one of which

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:29.290
is the concept and the
opportunity of sustainability,

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.200
at which food is at the
heart of the matter.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:36.464
Industrial food systems
are the common denominator.

00:02:36.464 --> 00:02:38.380
They sit, essentially,
as a common denominator

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.110
across a variety of factors
that actually directly

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:44.680
drive or contribute
to climate change.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.920
To name a few, there are
carbon emissions, pesticides

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.280
and pollution, deforestation,
and also water supply.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.220
And here's a taste
of what's at stake.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.530
One third of all
human-caused greenhouse

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.680
gas emissions is caused
by the global food system.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.340
And agricultural practices
are responsible for around 74%

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:06.311
of greenhouse gas nitrous
oxide in the United States.

00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:07.810
Now, what that does
is that actually

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.410
contributes to the rising global
temperatures across the world.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.210
With respect to
pesticides and pollution,

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:15.980
agriculture is the
largest contributor

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.770
of ammonia pollution and
other nitrogen compounds,

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:21.080
which compromises
our air quality.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.730
And pesticides
can be transported

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.010
in runoff and
groundwater and can

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.350
result in toxic levels
for humans and wildlife,

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:30.080
which then compromises
our water supply.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.550
And with respect
to deforestation,

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:36.230
75% of global deforestation
is caused by agriculture.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:37.814
And that's especially
problematic,

00:03:37.814 --> 00:03:39.980
given the stats they shared
around carbon emissions.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.040
Here we are, emitting even more
emissions into the atmosphere

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.800
and depleting the
natural resources that

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.560
act as a counterbalance--
the carbon stores, also known

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.100
as trees.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.070
Since 1960, one third of
the world's arable land

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:57.240
has been lost through erosion
and other land degradations.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.750
And with respect to water
supply, to support agriculture,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.310
rates of groundwater
withdrawal are

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:04.810
increasing relative to
water source replenishment

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:06.180
in the United States.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.250
That is, we are taking
more water from the Earth

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.620
than the Earth can
actually replenish.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.710
And by 2025, 1.8
billion people are

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.800
expected to live with
absolute water scarcity.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.860
Now, that does not just
impact agriculture or food.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.070
That impacts our
potential for life,

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.640
as water is crucial to life.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:28.960
So with all that
being said, where

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.420
does FarmLogix come into play?

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.450
They're continuing the
legacy of Earth Day

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:36.250
by fostering these new
responsible environmental

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.450
practices.

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:38.950
They're changing
the way that we eat

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.170
and they're also
supporting local farmers.

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.200
And that all begins
with the story of Linda.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.050
At age 40, she discovered
the thrill of the outdoors

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.970
and the beauty of nature.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.370
She was selected to
join the USA triathlon

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.775
team for her age group and
began racing across the country.

00:04:55.775 --> 00:04:58.150
It was on these well-worn
paths with the sun beating down

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:00.940
upon her back that she had the
opportunity to meet farmers,

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.430
to hear their stories,
to hear their strife

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.830
and their struggle,
their inherent desire

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:07.780
to live a green
and healthy life,

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.490
and also be compensated fairly
for the work that they do.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:12.870
She made an instant connection.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:16.020
And it was from that
point that she, in 2012,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.780
later on, actually
founded FarmLogix,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.930
naturally at her kitchen table,
a place of community, family,

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.300
storytelling, and nourishment.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.640
She applied her experience
in the futures industry

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.660
developing technology platforms
to the agricultural system

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.840
in order to build and scale
sustainable supply chains.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:35.790
Just one year later,
FarmLogix became

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.540
the farm-to-school solution for
Aramark Chicago Public Schools

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.000
and over 2,000
locations, overseeing

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.830
the sourcing of over $20
million worth of local foods.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.830
Now, today, FarmLogix provides
food technology services

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.650
nationwide with an eye towards
corporate responsibility,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:55.410
and actually offers
auditing services.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.560
They also manage active
procurement in over 15 states,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.450
and maintain an
e-commerce platform

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:01.950
for buyers and sellers.

00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:04.710
Now, these are all bullet
points in a timeline.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:07.710
But there is a narrative arc
that unites all of the above.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:09.140
And that is a story revolution.

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.680
That is a story of change
and a story of impact.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.400
That is a story around not only
changing the way that we eat

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.950
and empowering local farmers
through integrated cloud

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:19.410
computing solutions
provided by GCP,

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.590
but also bridging an
increasingly large gap

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:26.340
between people and food,
between people and nature,

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:28.800
and between people
and our home--

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:29.940
that is, the Earth.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:33.110
And that is truly in
the spirit of Earth Day.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:35.420
So with all that
being said, I would

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:38.450
like to welcome Linda to
begin her presentation.

00:06:38.450 --> 00:06:39.200
LINDA MALLERS: Hi.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:40.380
Thank you so much, Elisse.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.500
And thank you so much,
Google, for having me here

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:43.250
to tell our story.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:49.360
This is what a sustainable
supply chain looks like.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.250
But before I get
into the details,

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:53.620
I want to go back and tell
you a little bit about racing

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.480
and the connection
with the farmers.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.150
Some of the racing that I did
was called adventure racing,

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.240
where you race in
wilderness locations

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.920
with nothing but a pack on
your back for 48 to 72 hours.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.860
And you are in mother nature,
big, with all its glory

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.820
and all its challenges.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:17.260
But at the end of the day, I was
able to go home, put my racing

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:20.476
clothes away, take a shower,
and sign up for my next race.

00:07:20.476 --> 00:07:22.600
I learned through meeting
the farmers along the way

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.790
that farmers don't
have that opportunity.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.840
They are faced with the power of
mother nature every single day.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:36.340
So drought or rains or bounty
or not, that is their struggle.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:40.090
And that is something that
I related to very much.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:43.660
I found, really,
how disconnected

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:47.050
we had become from
our food system.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.160
We-- and myself included--

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:52.360
have really lost our connection
that there are many who

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.410
actually grow our food.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:55.750
And I realized that
they are our heroes.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.280
And I wanted to help them.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.010
And that is how
FarmLogix came to be.

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:03.280
I started out as a CSA
from my kitchen table.

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:06.850
And it developed into this
chart that you can see now.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:10.430
Basically, when local food was--

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:14.170
this is actually a real chart
about how farm to fork works

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.510
at Chicago Public Schools,
Aramark Chicago Public

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.550
Schools, which is one
of the largest school

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:20.290
districts in the country.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:23.260
And they have the largest
farm-to-school program

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:24.250
that we run.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.679
And basically, when
we came on board--

00:08:26.679 --> 00:08:28.720
you'll see that they have
four distributors there

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:29.920
on the chart.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.140
Each one of those distributors
was told, perhaps,

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:33.580
to source a local apple.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.480
And in doing that, they
would source from a state,

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.720
perhaps, maybe
from several farms.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:42.520
But basically, you had four
different sources going out

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.049
and looking for that food.

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:46.210
And a lot of the data
ended up getting lost.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.949
And we realized that.

00:08:47.949 --> 00:08:49.990
And it was really hard
for Chicago Public Schools

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:52.100
to be able to tell
any sort of story.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.540
So what we did was we created
a technology platform--

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:57.010
we call it Aggrega8--

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:00.140
that allows farmers to post
their availability online

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:03.250
so that large institutions,
like Chicago Public Schools,

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.740
can see what local
food is available

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.510
and actually order it.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:10.000
I put a red box around
logistics aggregation,

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.340
because that's the
role that we play.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:15.670
We come in there
and basically allow

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:19.180
the schools, universities,
health care systems, business

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:21.940
dining, such as maybe
your cafeteria here,

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:26.980
and sports venues to be able to
all order from the same farm.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:30.010
But we turn around and place
a single order with that farm.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.000
The technology places
a single order.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.080
So to the farmer, they know
they've sold an entire trailer

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:36.340
truck of apples.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.940
They may not know exactly
who's buying from them.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.080
But that's OK.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.160
We're really helping
them stay on the farm

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.660
and helping future
generations stay on the farm.

00:09:44.660 --> 00:09:47.812
We then turn around, and the
technology sells that product

00:09:47.812 --> 00:09:48.645
to the distributors.

00:09:48.645 --> 00:09:51.190
So the distributors
have just one customer,

00:09:51.190 --> 00:09:54.520
which is FarmLogix
technology platform,

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:56.950
and managed by our
technology platform.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.230
And really, what that does
is it keeps the distributor

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:02.500
from having to manage multiple
small farm relationships, which

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.350
is very complex, and was
hindering the movement.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.270
So we kind of sat
there in the middle.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:09.010
We call it Aggrega8.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:12.130
And Aggrega8 really
streamlines the whole process.

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:14.381
From there, we do
our data collection,

00:10:14.381 --> 00:10:16.630
which is the chart that you
see here, and run metrics.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:18.865
And those metrics are run
nationally and by location.

00:10:18.865 --> 00:10:20.864
And they're used by the
government, food service

00:10:20.864 --> 00:10:23.080
companies, distributors,
really to figure out

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:25.366
where is this food going,
and what is selling?

00:10:25.366 --> 00:10:26.740
And that really
helps the farmers

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.275
determine what to plant.

00:10:28.275 --> 00:10:30.400
On the far right, you'll
see this is a single day's

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:31.900
serving of carrots.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.700
It comes from Will
Allen's Growing Power

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:35.170
in Milwaukee, Wisconsin.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:38.620
36,000 pounds-- that
is one day's lunch.

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:42.640
And it feeds 350,000
kids at 650 schools.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.520
So really, to grow
the local food system,

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:49.400
you really have to think about
scale and meaningful impact.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:53.190
And this is how
we make it happen.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.160
So basically, responsible
sourcing at scale

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:59.010
requires equitable
access to healthy foods.

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:04.140
So we eventually, as
we started to scale,

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:07.350
went beyond the farm-to-school,
and started bringing that food

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:08.230
into the home.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:13.110
And so last year, we actually
won a grant as a team

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:16.120
with the Chicago Community Trust
and the Kinship Foundation.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.970
It was called the
Food-to-Market Challenge.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:20.860
And it was to create a CSA where
Chicago Public Schools were

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:22.745
actually pickup
locations for food that

00:11:22.745 --> 00:11:24.870
could be brought into the
home and not just enjoyed

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:26.530
at lunch at school.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:29.730
We now have several schools
as pickup locations,

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.160
and we've expanded that
into the health care sector.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.290
And we have, now, five
hospitals in Chicago

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:35.840
that are pickup locations.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:39.420
And we also work with
a retailer, Peapod.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.370
And we work with them
in multiple markets

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:44.620
running a CSA for them that's
everything is farm identified.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.040
So consumers can
order a CSA online,

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.250
and have it delivered to their
home to support local farms.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:52.200
We also have
farm-to-school, -campus,

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:54.750
and -workplace
programs that basically

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.450
allow people to enjoy local
foods when they're away

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:58.860
from home.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.410
Our consumer custom
e-commerce technology

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:03.820
reestablishes the fact
that we have a choice.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:11.220
I think what started to happen
when big ag came and we started

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:14.640
to scale for global
production, we lost our choice.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.640
And we started being told
what we were going to eat

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:18.960
and what was available.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:21.240
And I think, beginning
with Earth Day,

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:24.270
and then eventually, the
rise of the farmer's markets,

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:26.440
consumers realized they
really had a choice.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.530
And if they used
their voice, they

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.680
could have access to
healthy food away from home.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.700
And so really, our e-commerce
technology provides choice

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:38.760
and acknowledges that
the consumer has--

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.240
there's incredible
variations, like a thumbprint,

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.599
in what one considers
local and sustainable.

00:12:44.599 --> 00:12:46.140
And our e-commerce
allows you to shop

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.940
by what's important to you.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.670
And everything we do is
fully transparent at the time

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:51.210
of order.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.790
We provide farm profiles,
videos, and full source

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:57.110
transparency for storytelling.

00:12:57.110 --> 00:13:00.800
This is an example of an online
program that we run for Peapod.

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.280
As you can see, it's
called The Local Farm Box.

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.820
On the lower right is
an example of the box.

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:10.370
We also run other programs with
other products, such as meats.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:14.090
Everything comes with a recipe
and farm profiles as well.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.340
And then we provide, again,
the metrics on everything.

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:19.790
So you're looking at not
just produce and organics.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:22.280
You're looking at sustainable
and humanely-raised meats.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:25.970
You're looking at organic
dairy, cage-free eggs, bakery,

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:27.770
and other responsibly
sourced products.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:35.350
So we basically have three
aspects to our technology.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:37.480
We started out with e-commerce.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:41.620
And then we launched
Open Fields in 2017.

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:44.920
Open Fields basically
takes $6 billion

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.380
worth of purchasing data.

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:50.680
And we scrubbed it by 85
sustainable attributes.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.260
So those attributes
run into categories

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:57.490
such as ecologically sound, fair
trade, human health, locally

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:59.470
sourced, and even seafood.

00:13:59.470 --> 00:14:02.560
And basically, what we do is we
provide this custom searching

00:14:02.560 --> 00:14:04.780
experience through Open Fields.

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:07.270
So somebody can go
online and say, show me

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:09.820
what is 15 miles from
me that is organic.

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:13.120
Show me humanely raised
meats within my state.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:17.140
We have custom definitions of
local by mileage radius, state,

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:20.570
combinations of state
or even state programs.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:24.940
We also provide compliance
with sustainability programs,

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:26.980
such as AASHE STARS,
which is the American

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:30.400
Association for Sustainability
in Higher Ed, Real Food

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.290
Challenge, and Health
Care Without Harm.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:36.310
And these programs have
requirements and auditing

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:37.310
that is required.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:39.850
And so our search
tool allows you

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:42.940
to search for compliant
products for those programs.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.590
And just a plug for Google
here-- at the bottom,

00:14:45.590 --> 00:14:47.380
you'll see that, in
our platform, also,

00:14:47.380 --> 00:14:50.650
is the ability to generate
Google Maps to show

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:51.850
the location of the farm.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.160
And it also displays the
mileage to the location

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:55.220
of the person searching.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:00.340
So if you think
of Open Fields, is

00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:02.440
we consider Open
Fields the possible.

00:15:02.440 --> 00:15:04.150
And the possible
is really what's

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:06.822
out there that's available to
purchase but has not been--

00:15:06.822 --> 00:15:08.530
well, has been purchased
by someone else,

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:11.590
but not nationally purchased
by the person searching.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:15.040
Open Fields reporting is
a post-transaction product

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:18.010
that we have that
allows any location that

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:20.470
uses our services to
be able to run metrics

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.720
on their sustainable purchasing.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.600
Often, you'll find a
distributor or a company that

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:28.490
is selling a product,
will provide reporting,

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:30.490
but when you really
think about it,

00:15:30.490 --> 00:15:32.540
somebody like the
University of Virginia,

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:36.610
which is highlighted here, will
use 20, 30 different sources.

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:39.820
So to get that whole
holistic reporting,

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:43.810
you really need to
do data collection

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:45.040
from all your purchasing.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:47.620
So we basically take
that data collection,

00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:50.320
and then we provide our
clients with a dashboard

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.780
from which they can tell
their sustainable story.

00:15:52.780 --> 00:15:55.630
And again, just like you can
search by custom metrics,

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:58.640
you can run your reports by
customer metrics as well.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.520
So this is basically
a STARS report

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:03.220
for the University of Virginia,
all their locations, all

00:16:03.220 --> 00:16:04.690
their purchasing.

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.630
And one thing to note
is that since there

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:10.330
is no single definition
for responsible sourcing

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:13.330
in the food space, these reports
can be run multiple times

00:16:13.330 --> 00:16:14.656
for multiple reasons.

00:16:14.656 --> 00:16:16.780
Some people might want to
run a full sustainability

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:18.160
report and a STARS report.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:21.370
And we provide that
complete flexibility.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:26.450
We also can offer the ability
to do very large scale metrics--

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:30.850
oops-- metrics across an entire
enterprise, the entire country,

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:32.530
et cetera.

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:35.230
Aggrega8 is our
e-commerce platform.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:37.990
You had looked at the
flow chart for that.

00:16:37.990 --> 00:16:42.130
And so Aggrega8, this is what
it looks like when a purchase is

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:43.450
actually happening.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:46.390
An institution would
go online to place,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:49.510
let's say, the apples
that you see on the left.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.660
The buyer would see the
price, the pack size, which

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:54.100
is important to institutions.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:57.070
Things are sold in 20
pound cases, usually.

00:16:57.070 --> 00:16:59.820
They would see the farm
that is growing the product.

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:01.570
They would see the
miles to their location

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:04.322
to see if it complies with
their definition of local.

00:17:04.322 --> 00:17:06.280
If you look at the top,
you will see that there

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.800
are tabs for days of the week.

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:11.890
What we do is we use this as
an advanced ordering system,

00:17:11.890 --> 00:17:15.010
where multiple,
multiple institutions--

00:17:15.010 --> 00:17:18.760
60, 70, perhaps-- in a region
will go online and order

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.710
by the week a month in advance.

00:17:20.710 --> 00:17:23.560
And then what we do is we
add up all of that demand

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.140
and give a single
order to a farm

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:29.410
with what they need to
have available each week.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:32.410
And then basically, on the
other side of Aggrega8,

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:35.350
distributors are made aware of
when the product is coming in

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:37.960
and when the customer
is expecting it.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.240
Believe it or not,
Aggrega8 is a tool

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:45.170
that is being used now
by the nonprofit world.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:47.495
We just formed a collaboration
with the National Farm

00:17:47.495 --> 00:17:50.410
to School Network, and we'll
be launching a pilot in June

00:17:50.410 --> 00:17:51.610
in Illinois.

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:53.290
And basically,
what will happen is

00:17:53.290 --> 00:17:56.860
we will be using our reporting
tools to gather information

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:59.320
on their farm-to-school
activity in Illinois.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:01.930
And then we will
identify common products

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.700
that all school
districts are buying,

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:05.530
and create an Aggrega8
platform for them

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:08.230
to scale the farm-to-school
movement in Illinois

00:18:08.230 --> 00:18:09.490
through the use of Aggrega8.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:13.400
So we're very
excited about that.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:15.050
Behind the scenes
in Aggrega8, are

00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:18.620
tools for farms to become
food safety compliant.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:22.220
One thing to-- the
law is the law,

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.470
and the Food Safety
Modernization Act

00:18:24.470 --> 00:18:28.820
requires farms and will require
farms, regardless of size,

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:30.999
to be able to comply
with food safety.

00:18:30.999 --> 00:18:33.290
So basically, they will have
to say when they harvested

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:36.620
their product, the date
that they packed it,

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.810
who picked it up,
when it was delivered,

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:40.400
and then what
source it came from.

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:43.310
And that could be a
farm or even a field.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:45.590
So Aggrega8 provides
this behind-the-scenes

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:46.760
for the farmer.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:51.020
We provide it free to any
farmer that works in our system,

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:51.740
basically.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:54.260
But also, food hubs
and cooperatives

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:56.690
can use it to manage their
own business, almost as

00:18:56.690 --> 00:18:58.610
a small ERP system.

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:02.750
Basically, then, from that
data, labels can be generated.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.210
And those labels then
can be put on the boxes

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:07.100
with barcodes for
full compliance

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:08.240
with large distributors.

00:19:08.240 --> 00:19:11.000
So basically, we allow
that small farmer

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.970
to play in a much bigger
space by providing them

00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:15.290
with the food safety.

00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:17.390
And this is super important.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:20.000
There was actually
a, as you know,

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.850
last week, an E. coli break out.

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:25.190
And it's interesting.

00:19:25.190 --> 00:19:29.330
They've identified where that
Romaine lettuce actually grew.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.760
But I think you can
tell that it was

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:35.000
a very large grower,
because there

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:36.641
were outbreaks in 11 states.

00:19:36.641 --> 00:19:39.140
That tells you that that's a
large grower, when that product

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:40.850
is getting into 11 states.

00:19:40.850 --> 00:19:43.490
So when you do purchase
locally, and you

00:19:43.490 --> 00:19:44.990
know where that
food is coming from,

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:47.850
you get a label like
this on your box.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:51.660
And it's just a much
more contained issue.

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:54.320
So that's another reason
to purchase local.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:01.700
And then just bigger
picture, all of this activity

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:04.400
gets entered into our
database, obviously.

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.734
Again, more than $1 billion
of sustainable transactions

00:20:08.734 --> 00:20:09.900
have run through our system.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:11.520
And that continues to grow.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:15.030
But we're able to turn
around and deliver metrics

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.870
to large food service management
companies, distributors,

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:20.420
nonprofits, and the
government as well.

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:22.460
And we do that by a
variety of metrics

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:25.760
that you can see on the screen--
location, farm, SKU, spend,

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.425
fruit category, and product.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:32.720
So just real quickly, I'll
talk about the timeline,

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:35.290
because we're going to get
into the tech a little bit now.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:38.890
In 2013-14, I
personally launched this

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:43.690
at my kitchen table using
three off-the-shelf tools.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:45.760
And I called myself
Oz behind the curtain,

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:48.370
because it looked great,
but it was a lot of work.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:52.000
And then in 2014, we started
to create a proprietary program

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.590
in MySQL.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.140
And we launched
our four products--

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:59.252
CloudSlot, Open Fields,
Aggrega8, and MaetaData.

00:20:59.252 --> 00:21:00.460
And that's not a misspelling.

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:02.800
That was my grandmother's name.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:08.980
In 2017, we migrated to Google
BigQuery for our reporting

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:13.240
platform, and are currently
operating on multiple clouds.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:16.240
In 2018, we have
plans to integrate

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:19.030
to Google Cloud SQL and API.

00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:23.560
And then in 2019, we have plans
to integrate to Elasticsearch

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:27.210
and add Google Retail Solutions.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.490
So a little bit
about how that looks.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:34.020
Right now, you'll see the MY
SQL on the upper left in the AWS

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:35.260
environment.

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:38.370
Nightly now, we
export that data.

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.260
That goes to the
Google Cloud Server

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.990
and down into
BigQuery, where we do

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:48.540
manual file exports of large
size for enterprise metrics.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:54.060
And then we use Jasper Soft and
Power BI for individual metrics

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:56.460
and reports that you saw,
similar to the University

00:21:56.460 --> 00:21:57.630
of Virginia.

00:21:57.630 --> 00:21:59.950
Currently, we're using
Algolia for our search engine

00:21:59.950 --> 00:22:02.130
and Jasper Soft
for our reporting,

00:22:02.130 --> 00:22:05.046
in addition to
Microsoft Power BI.

00:22:05.046 --> 00:22:07.420
And we were one of your first
beta customers for BigQuery

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:09.880
to Microsoft Power BI.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:13.900
And by the way, we use
the consulting firm

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.355
Aptitive in Chicago to do this.

00:22:15.355 --> 00:22:17.480
This was their recommendation,
to move to BigQuery,

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:18.730
and we're really appreciative.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:20.090
They did a great job.

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:20.590
OK.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:26.560
So 2018 sees us starting
to migrate from MySQL.

00:22:26.560 --> 00:22:29.440
And so we will be doing
that, and we're actually

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:31.930
doing that currently,
to Google Cloud Server.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:33.820
And what that's going
to allow us to do

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.070
is really scale our business.

00:22:36.070 --> 00:22:38.620
MySQL was really never
meant to run $6 billion

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.300
worth of purchasing data.

00:22:40.300 --> 00:22:42.875
It really was meant to build
an e-commerce platform.

00:22:42.875 --> 00:22:44.500
And we didn't really
see this direction

00:22:44.500 --> 00:22:46.210
when we launched the company.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:50.380
So Google Cloud SQL is going to
be able to allow us to scale.

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:52.900
And then that data
will go into BigQuery,

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:57.430
and also go into our
Aggrega8 commerce system.

00:22:57.430 --> 00:22:59.495
So we're really
excited about that.

00:22:59.495 --> 00:23:01.870
The next thing, number two,
that you can see on the chart

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:05.530
is we also are going to start
accepting contributed data.

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:07.060
And that would be
purchasing data

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:08.980
from outside sources,
small vendors that

00:23:08.980 --> 00:23:10.660
don't make it into
the system, farmers

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:12.140
that have product to sell.

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.930
So we basically
are transitioning

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:18.050
to a full true marketplace.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:21.630
2019, you see us
also transitioning

00:23:21.630 --> 00:23:26.280
to Google Elasticsearch,
which would possibly

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:31.200
replace Algolia for us so that
there's more full integration.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:33.870
We'll probably also
use your Kibana tool,

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.980
and then also transition to
using Google Cloud and BigQuery

00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:40.320
for our e-commerce
as well, which would

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:42.820
be a whole environment for us.

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:47.090
And the idea of that
is very, very exciting.

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:50.310
So why did we choose
Google Cloud Platform?

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:52.970
Well, Aptitive chose
it for us first.

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:54.820
But why do we love it?

00:23:54.820 --> 00:23:57.070
The open platform
integrates seamlessly

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.360
with our MySQL database.

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.170
So there were no issues
making a transition.

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:04.670
The migration was
quick and easy.

00:24:04.670 --> 00:24:07.780
We started in late November, and
we were done by the holidays.

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.010
So it was wonderful.

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:10.870
It was about 30 days.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:12.670
And we had complete
visibility that

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:14.990
was not possible with MySQL.

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:17.620
The dataprep is intuitive
and transparent.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:20.540
And there's many of us
that access and use it.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:23.757
BigQuery is compatible with
a wide variety of reporting

00:24:23.757 --> 00:24:25.840
solutions that we're
beginning to experiment with,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.640
which is exciting.

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:31.210
The flexible structure allows us
to grow when the time is right.

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:33.040
The managed services
and security

00:24:33.040 --> 00:24:35.890
allow us to grow faster than
our learning curve sometimes.

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.690
And Elasticsearch
and Retail allows

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:40.240
us to run our apps
from a single database.

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:48.850
So just at a glance,
obviously, technology

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:51.820
scales all sorts of things.

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:54.730
But in our space, what
is really exciting

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.020
is that it's scaling a movement.

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:59.350
It's scaling a
true movement to be

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:02.740
able to put together
and scale and really

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:05.710
have impact, not
just on local farms,

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:08.020
but equal access
to healthy foods

00:25:08.020 --> 00:25:10.960
in all neighborhoods
in rural neighborhoods,

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:13.180
urban neighborhoods.

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:16.160
We all go to restaurants and
eat local food on the menu.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:19.090
But it's exciting to us
when we see that food moving

00:25:19.090 --> 00:25:20.830
into low income
communities, communities

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:22.120
that don't have
grocery stores, when

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.661
you see people having access to
healthy food and their health

00:25:24.661 --> 00:25:25.510
improving.

00:25:25.510 --> 00:25:27.700
So basically, what
Google is doing

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:29.930
for us is allowing us
to scale a movement.

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:32.980
And so we see technology
as a tool in that regard,

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:34.330
and we're very grateful.

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:35.950
Just some stats on us--

00:25:35.950 --> 00:25:45.650
we've saved 1,500 metric
tons of CO2 since inception.

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:47.600
And our technology
allows buyers to source

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:50.540
by custom mileage parameters.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:54.890
Small farms producing for fruits
and vegetables consumption

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:57.470
usually don't use
harmful pesticides.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:01.760
That is usually used to
grow food not for humans.

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.252
So the small
farmers that you see

00:26:03.252 --> 00:26:05.210
on the roadside at your
farmer's market usually

00:26:05.210 --> 00:26:07.040
don't use those pesticides.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:12.350
And so our system now
identifies 12 criteria

00:26:12.350 --> 00:26:15.020
for ecologically sound,
certified organic foods,

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:17.960
et cetera, that support
these small farms.

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:19.850
One thing that's
super important is

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.580
that the rise of
superbugs in this country

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:25.550
is due to the antibiotic
resistance compounded

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.870
by the antibiotics that
we eat in our food.

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:30.690
It isn't because we have
taken too many antibiotics.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:33.020
It's because we eat
too many antibiotics.

00:26:33.020 --> 00:26:36.080
And there are a lot of poultry
producers and pork producers

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.960
who are now realizing this,
and really transitioning--

00:26:38.960 --> 00:26:40.940
and we commend them--

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:43.100
to being fully no
antibiotic ever.

00:26:43.100 --> 00:26:45.470
It's actually called NAE.

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:48.240
And our system identifies
products that are NAE.

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:49.940
And we actually
run an NAE program

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:52.190
through Chicago Public Schools.

00:26:52.190 --> 00:26:55.220
And then the average age of
the American farmer is 58.

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:57.830
And what we really want is
we want the next generation

00:26:57.830 --> 00:26:59.100
to want to stay on the farm.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:02.660
We want farming to be
a viable occupation.

00:27:02.660 --> 00:27:05.970
We want there to be abundance.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:07.610
And we want there to be hope.

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:10.610
And so really, that
is why we're here.

00:27:10.610 --> 00:27:13.410
And basically, what our
farm-to-school programs,

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:16.490
our farm-to-cafeteria programs,
our CSA programs, really are

00:27:16.490 --> 00:27:19.070
create that real scalable
model for the future

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:20.000
so that we all thrive.

00:27:22.607 --> 00:27:23.940
ELISSE ROCHE: And that's a wrap.

00:27:23.940 --> 00:27:25.310
Well, thank you, Linda.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:27.290
So before we dive
into the Q&amp;A, I've

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:31.310
have actually prepared a few
questions myself for Linda.

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:34.190
So to start, how would
you define local?

00:27:34.190 --> 00:27:36.350
And how does that
definition come into play

00:27:36.350 --> 00:27:39.140
when serving communities
around the United States?

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:40.280
LINDA MALLERS: Yeah.

00:27:40.280 --> 00:27:42.590
Well, my personal
definition really

00:27:42.590 --> 00:27:46.790
doesn't matter, because local
is in the eye of the beholder.

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:48.530
So for some people,
it might be what's

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:51.710
down the road, what's within
50 miles of me, what's

00:27:51.710 --> 00:27:53.880
in my state, what's in
the states touching me.

00:27:53.880 --> 00:27:56.060
And that's actually
somebody's definition.

00:27:56.060 --> 00:27:57.740
The state of Rhode
Island's definition

00:27:57.740 --> 00:27:59.510
is the state of Rhode Island.

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:03.170
So really, what we do is we
allow folks to both search

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.847
and purchase by what
their definition is,

00:28:05.847 --> 00:28:07.430
and then for the
reporting and metrics

00:28:07.430 --> 00:28:10.340
to be run by what our
clients' definition is.

00:28:10.340 --> 00:28:12.890
For instance, Aramark's
definition is 250.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:15.200
But a lot of their clients,
it's not their definition.

00:28:15.200 --> 00:28:17.751
And so we really allow for
that custom definition.

00:28:17.751 --> 00:28:18.250
Yeah.

00:28:18.250 --> 00:28:19.790
ELISSE ROCHE: That's great.

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:22.310
And originally, you
started out by creating

00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:24.470
sustainable and
local food systems.

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:27.470
Now, you collect
national scale food data.

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:30.572
How does that data drive
consumer decisions?

00:28:30.572 --> 00:28:32.030
LINDA MALLERS:
Well, I think what's

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:36.770
really interesting about our
database is that we did not

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:39.024
realize, when we started
collecting this data,

00:28:39.024 --> 00:28:40.940
that there's a lot that
the consumer is buying

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:42.500
that's already local
and sustainable

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:44.420
and they didn't know it.

00:28:44.420 --> 00:28:47.030
So I think what's happening
is it's exciting for people

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:48.830
to see, oh, I didn't
realize that that

00:28:48.830 --> 00:28:50.360
was grown in Michigan.

00:28:50.360 --> 00:28:53.000
I didn't realize that that
chicken that I'm eating

00:28:53.000 --> 00:28:55.280
has no antibiotics in it.

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:57.710
And so really,
what we're doing is

00:28:57.710 --> 00:28:59.780
we're creating
opportunities for education.

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:03.728
And an educated consumer
will drive the movement.

00:29:03.728 --> 00:29:05.180
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:07.610
And in today's
complicated food system,

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.870
how can the average
person drive change?

00:29:09.870 --> 00:29:11.480
How can someone
navigate the variety

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:14.870
of labels and classifications
to make more environmentally

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:16.052
conscious choices?

00:29:16.052 --> 00:29:18.260
LINDA MALLERS: Well, I'd
say that, obviously, there's

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:19.970
a lot of labeling that we see.

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:22.490
And there's a lot of labeling
we see that isn't true.

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.320
And so I think that there's
legislation being done by that,

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:28.370
and that's very,
very important work.

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:31.249
But as consumers, I think what
we have to do is use our voice.

00:29:31.249 --> 00:29:32.040
ELISSE ROCHE: Yeah.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.248
LINDA MALLERS: I think that
what's really interesting

00:29:34.248 --> 00:29:37.190
is we see the loudest voices
on college campuses today.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:39.740
And really, what's
happening is, rather than

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:43.370
demand local food
on a menu, they

00:29:43.370 --> 00:29:44.900
are demanding it
everywhere they go.

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:46.441
They want it on
their college campus.

00:29:46.441 --> 00:29:48.527
They want it in their dorms.

00:29:48.527 --> 00:29:50.360
Eventually, when they
go out into the world,

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:52.930
they want it at work.

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:55.520
And you see people,
even health care--

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.110
people wanting to
make food choices

00:29:57.110 --> 00:29:59.760
when they're in the
hospital, anywhere they are.

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:03.560
And so I think that what we
can do is keep using our voice,

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.290
because everybody's individual
voice is getting pretty darn

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:06.971
loud.

00:30:06.971 --> 00:30:07.470
Yeah.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:09.290
ELISSE ROCHE: That's awesome.

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:13.160
And looking ahead to 2030, at
the 17 Sustainable Development

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:16.460
Goals developed by the UN,
sustainable food systems

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.490
are necessary to achieve
those goals, like zero hunger

00:30:19.490 --> 00:30:21.770
and responsible consumption.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:24.080
What would you say are
critical practices that

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:27.032
must be continued or adopted
in order to reach those goals?

00:30:27.032 --> 00:30:28.490
LINDA MALLERS:
Well, I think what's

00:30:28.490 --> 00:30:31.880
really important is to recognize
the entire supply chain needs

00:30:31.880 --> 00:30:33.770
to be involved.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:35.400
I was reading those
goals this morning.

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:40.250
And in order to eradicate
poverty and hunger and these

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:43.280
very large issues that
we have-- disease--

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:45.930
you really have to engage
the whole supply chain.

00:30:45.930 --> 00:30:48.830
And we have to work together,
and we have to collaborate.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:51.110
Nobody can be thrown
under the bus.

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:53.660
Often, we see large
companies in this space

00:30:53.660 --> 00:30:56.650
being thrown under the bus
just for being large companies.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:31:00.740
But it's large institutional
buyers and large distributors

00:31:00.740 --> 00:31:03.470
that have the ability and the
customers to buy this food

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:04.680
and move this food.

00:31:04.680 --> 00:31:06.650
And so we all have
to play together.

00:31:06.650 --> 00:31:08.400
And that's how we
see real change.

00:31:08.400 --> 00:31:09.149
Yeah.

00:31:09.149 --> 00:31:10.190
ELISSE ROCHE: Absolutely.

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:11.990
Well, that is certainly
an inspiring vision

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:13.469
for the future.

00:31:13.469 --> 00:31:16.010
And now, with that, I would like
to thank you again, and move

00:31:16.010 --> 00:31:18.960
to the live Q&amp;A. We'll be
back in less than a minute.

00:31:49.710 --> 00:31:52.230
So now, we have some
questions from the audience.

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:54.900
To start, what was the
initial prototyping process

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:57.289
like for Aggrega8?

00:31:57.289 --> 00:31:59.580
LINDA MALLERS: It makes me
laugh reading that question.

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:01.110
That was five years ago.

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:06.494
So basically, we used just
an off-the-shelf e-commerce

00:32:06.494 --> 00:32:07.535
platform called Volusion.

00:32:07.535 --> 00:32:09.600
You can go to volusion.com.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:12.170
We still operate
some of those today.

00:32:12.170 --> 00:32:14.190
And basically, but it
was really interesting,

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:20.940
because typical e-commerce
cannot sustain the growth,

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.350
mostly because you look
at pictures of product,

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.260
and a lot of people buying in
the institutional space want

00:32:26.260 --> 00:32:27.610
to look at lists.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:30.330
So then we developed a
list on another platform,

00:32:30.330 --> 00:32:33.180
and then we would move
that data over to Volusion.

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:35.740
And I'm telling you,
it was very funny.

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:38.760
So I would say that it
began with very, very

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.320
basic e-commerce.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.150
And then what we
really did is we

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:45.420
waited until we knew where
all the supply chain gaps were

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:47.850
and what existed
out there before we

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:50.470
created our own platform.

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:52.230
But that was what
the prototyping was.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:53.520
It was barely.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:58.458
ELISSE ROCHE: You've
come a long way.

00:32:58.458 --> 00:32:59.465
LINDA MALLERS: Yeah.

00:32:59.465 --> 00:33:00.840
ELISSE ROCHE: And
second question

00:33:00.840 --> 00:33:04.020
is what's the greatest issue
facing farmers today that you

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:06.360
think technology can solve?

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:09.570
LINDA MALLERS: Well, it depends
on which issue they face.

00:33:09.570 --> 00:33:12.540
There are tons of tech
solutions out there

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:16.050
in the growing
space to help them--

00:33:16.050 --> 00:33:18.060
I actually just read
an article about you

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:20.640
all, that Google was
doing some research

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:24.292
on being able to alert farmers
of future weather issues

00:33:24.292 --> 00:33:26.250
that might impact them
and how they can protect

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:28.540
themselves, which is terrific.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:31.110
So we see a lot
of tech solutions

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:35.790
on the growing side to help
protect farmers and help

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:40.350
them scale, help them harvest
more efficiently, et cetera.

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:44.640
I think that the space we occupy
and where we think we help--

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:47.160
and there's not that
much participation there

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:48.780
on the technology
side right now--

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:51.230
is in bringing their
products to market.

00:33:51.230 --> 00:33:53.730
We're a firm
believer that farmers

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:54.850
need to focus on growing.

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:56.058
That's what they're great at.

00:33:56.058 --> 00:33:58.140
And we like to have
our technology take

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:00.522
care of the rest.

00:34:00.522 --> 00:34:01.980
ELISSE ROCHE: And
the last question

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:06.780
that we have from the audience
is what are FarmLogix's most

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.831
important metrics and KPIs?

00:34:08.831 --> 00:34:10.830
LINDA MALLERS: Well, I
would say, you know what?

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:13.070
Again, it's in the
eye of the beholder

00:34:13.070 --> 00:34:15.750
and what they want to report on.

00:34:15.750 --> 00:34:18.909
But I would say I think
what's most important is when

00:34:18.909 --> 00:34:21.790
we create that baseline
for a customer,

00:34:21.790 --> 00:34:23.790
when we're in the corporate
responsibility space

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:26.460
working on a project, where we
develop that baseline and say,

00:34:26.460 --> 00:34:30.310
here's where you are today,
and where do you want to be?

00:34:30.310 --> 00:34:34.620
And then we help them not
only measure but also identify

00:34:34.620 --> 00:34:36.940
where there are gaps and
rooms for improvement.

00:34:36.940 --> 00:34:39.810
And so some of that
is going to be maybe

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:42.719
you should change your
seafood purchasing practices,

00:34:42.719 --> 00:34:47.909
or obviously, produce is less
expensive than sustainable

00:34:47.909 --> 00:34:48.420
meats.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:50.711
And so maybe you want to add
some of that into the mix.

00:34:50.711 --> 00:34:55.110
And so really, the metrics
are the baseline, and then

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:57.316
the goal and the achievement.

00:34:57.316 --> 00:34:58.440
ELISSE ROCHE: That's great.

00:34:58.440 --> 00:34:59.849
Well, those are
all the questions

00:34:59.849 --> 00:35:01.140
that we have from the audience.

